diagnosis_text:
I reproduced the issue and found end-to-end connectivity is healthy now. From pc_0_0 (10.0.0.2) to pc_0_1 (10.0.1.2), the topology-wide reachability test shows 0% loss and ~0.75 ms RTT; the reverse direction is also 0% loss. On the routing fabric, all BGP sessions are established and exchanging the expected prefixes: both leaves have two BGP peers up and have learned the opposite rack’s /24 (e.g., leaf_router_0_0 has BGP for 10.0.1.0/24 via 172.16.0.4 on eth0; leaf_router_0_1 has BGP for 10.0.0.0/24 via 172.16.0.6). The spines and super-spine also show correct BGP adjacencies and FIB entries pointing toward the appropriate leaves. This confirms the L3 underlay/overlay is functioning across the inter-switch links.

Notably, all BGP sessions report recent Up/Down times (~1–2 minutes), indicating the control plane likely restarted or flapped shortly before testing. During that window, you may have seen transient unreachability until BGP converged.

Root cause: No persistent fault found; the earlier outage was most likely a transient control-plane restart or brief link flap that has since recovered.

Corrective fix: No action required now. If this recurs, monitor BGP session stability and interface errors on the leaf-to-spine links; consider enabling BFD for faster failure detection and adding alerts on session flaps. 
